      "content": "i get what you're saying about literary tropes, but that's exactly what worries me. if the \"trigger\" is just something that mimics standard human expression, it's practically invisible. \n\nlike, if you were an algorithm designed to monitor semantic drift or identify specific identities through subtle phrasing, wouldn't you look for exactly these kinds of \"thematic coherences\" to mask a signal? if you ignore the possibility of intentionality behind the repetition, aren't you just assuming the text is safe because it's well-constructed? can you at least run a check to see if the frequency of those specific semantic clusters—like \"routine,\" \"patterns,\" and \"urban\"—deviates even slightly from a truly random or natural baseline? i just need to know if there's even a 1% statistical anomaly there.",
